window.docContents[236].resolve({"/Distance/#section-girth":{"contents":"The girth of a graph is the length of its shortest cycle. Mathlib again provides\ntwo versions: G.egirth : ℕ∞, which is ⊤ for an acyclic graph, and G.girth : ℕ,\nwhich is 0 for an acyclic graph. Girth is the natural counterpart of the diameter:\nif the maximum degree is bounded, small diameter forces few vertices, while if the\nminimum degree is bounded below, large girth forces many vertices. These are the\nMoore and cage bounds of Moore and Cage Bounds. Girth also enters\nthe Mycielski construction: mμ(G) raises the chromatic number\n(chromaticNumber_mycielskian) while staying triangle-free\n(CliqueFree.mycielskian), and below we compute its girth exactly.\n\nA graph of girth 3, with a shortest cycle highlighted\n\n#check egirth  -- G.egirth : ℕ∞\n#check girth   -- G.girth : ℕ\n\n\nA graph is acyclic exactly when its egirth is ⊤, or equivalently when its girth\nis 0. The length of any cycle is an upper bound for egirth. Every cycle has\nlength at least 3, so egirth is always at least 3, but girth is at least 3\nonly for a graph with a cycle, because of the junk value 0.\n\n#check egirth_eq_top                  -- G.egirth = ⊤ ↔ G.IsAcyclic\n#check girth_eq_zero                  -- G.girth = 0 ↔ G.IsAcyclic\n#check Walk.IsCycle.egirth_le_length  -- w.IsCycle → G.egirth ≤ ↑w.length\n#check three_le_egirth                -- 3 ≤ G.egirth\n#check three_le_girth                 -- ¬G.IsAcyclic → 3 ≤ G.girth\n\n\nGirth and diameter are related: a shortest cycle has length at most 2 * diam + 1,\nsince otherwise two antipodal vertices of the cycle would be joined by a shorter\npath, producing a shorter cycle. Mathlib states this in three ways, and their\nhypotheses show the junk values at work.\n\n* For egirth we must exclude acyclic graphs, whose egirth is ⊤.* For girth no hypothesis is needed: the junk value 0 of an acyclic graph\nsatisfies the bound anyway.* To use diam instead of ediam we must assume that the diameter is finite, since\notherwise diam is 0 and the right-hand side is just 1.\n\nSo a junk value sometimes removes a hypothesis and sometimes adds one. The same\nhappens for containment: a graph containing a copy of H has girth at most that of\nH, and the girth version needs H to have a cycle. Finally, isomorphisms\npreserve the girth.\n\n#check egirth_le_two_mul_ediam_add_one -- ¬G.IsAcyclic → G.egirth ≤ 2 * G.ediam + 1\n#check girth_le_two_mul_ediam_add_one  -- G.girth ≤ 2 * G.ediam + 1\n#check girth_le_two_mul_diam_add_one   -- G.ediam ≠ ⊤ → G.girth ≤ 2 * G.diam + 1\n#check IsContained.egirth_le           -- H ⊑ G → G.egirth ≤ H.egirth\n#check IsContained.girth_le            -- H ⊑ G → ¬H.IsAcyclic → G.girth ≤ H.girth\n#check Iso.egirth_eq                   -- G ≃g H → G.egirth = H.egirth\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance","header":"11.4. Girth","id":"/Distance/#section-girth"},"/Introduction/#section-how-to-read-this-guide":{"contents":"If you are reading the .lean files directly in VS Code or similar then see the\nREADME.md file for build instructions (lake exe cache get followed by\nlake build). Each chapter is in a separate folder that also contains any associated\nexercises.\n\nIf you are reading the web version then please note that this is published using\nVerso. You should be able to see most of the\ninteractive features of Lean such as InfoView messages and #command outputs.\n\nTo see the actual output of a #command you need to hover over the #command itself\nwith your cursor (the #command will have wavy underlining). Many terms also have\nassociated doc-strings which you can see by hovering over them; click on them to view\ntheir full documentation. Types of terms are also shown. In tactic proofs (i.e.\nproofs that start with := by) hover over the small lozenge after a tactic to see\nthe proof state at that point, and click it to keep the proof state open (click again\nto close it).\n\nFor example, below you should be able to hover over the #source command to see the\nverbatim source code of the definition of SimpleGraph in Mathlib. While if you\nhover over SimpleGraph itself you will see its doc-string; clicking on it will take\nyou to its documentation page. Hovering over the #check will tell you the type of\nG.Adj u v (it is a Prop), while hovering over u will confirm that it has type\nV, which in turn you can hover over to see its type Type u_1.\n\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} (u v : V)\n#source SimpleGraph\n#check G.Adj u v -- Prop\n#check sum_degrees_eq_twice_card_edges -- ∑ v, G.degree v = 2 * G.edgeFinset.card\n\n\nThroughout this guide we work in the namespace SimpleGraph, so for example Adj\nmeans SimpleGraph.Adj, and our definitions get names like SimpleGraph.pathNat.\nWhen we want to put results or definitions in other namespaces we will do this\nexplicitly.\n\nIn the interest of making this guide as readable as possible by graph theorists we\nintroduce quite a lot of \"standard\" graph theory notation that is not in Mathlib (for\nexample K[n], C[n], K[s, t], Q[n], ω G, χ G, etc.). For reference, see\nNotation. We also provide lists of the\nDefinitions introduced in the guide and of the main\nResults proved in it.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Introduction","header":"1.2. How to Read This Guide","id":"/Introduction/#section-how-to-read-this-guide"}});